say which kern_return a timed semaphore wait answered

libdispatch retries on KERN_ABORTED, returns on KERN_OPERATION_TIMED_OUT and TRAPS on anything
else, so a fourth value is a SIGILL inside _dispatch_sema4_timedwait with nothing said about
why. iTerm2 dies there while building its windows.

Reported once, with the port name, the timeout and the pid, because the value is the whole
question. Measured: name=20483 with a 30ms timeout answers 15, KERN_INVALID_NAME, for a name
that is plainly not zero.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
@@ -563,6 +563,22 @@
 		__simple_abort();
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * WHAT WE HAND BACK MATTERS TO THE CALLER, and only three answers are legal here. libdispatch
+	 * retries on KERN_ABORTED, returns on KERN_OPERATION_TIMED_OUT, and TRAPS on anything else, so
+	 * a fourth value is a SIGILL in _dispatch_sema4_timedwait with nothing said about why. iTerm2
+	 * dies there. Reported once, because the value is the whole question.
+	 */
+	if (code != KERN_SUCCESS && code != KERN_ABORTED && code != 49 /* KERN_OPERATION_TIMED_OUT */) {
+		static int reported = 0;
+
+		if (!reported) {
+			reported = 1;
+			__simple_printf("CIDER_SEM pid=%d semaphore_timedwait(name=%d, %us %uns) answered %d, which libdispatch will trap on\n",
+			                (int) getpid(), (int) wait_name, (unsigned) sec, (unsigned) nsec, code);
+		}
+	}
+
 	return code;
 }
 
